abstract: >-
  I didn't invent a machine. I found one. That is my conviction. The Thiele
  Machine is how I put it on the table: an executable argument about which
  distinctions an account of computation should preserve. A computation gives
  you an answer. What did it establish on the way there? Which distinctions
  did it keep? What did it cost to establish them? Certification is one point
  I could pin down. The machine carries structural state, a cost ledger, and
  rules for particular events. Its proofs show exactly what specified
  observations lose. A full encoding can preserve those distinctions; the
  question is which ones our models should keep. The formal development
  includes accounting laws for systems satisfying A2, pricing adequacy for
  designated events, ledger uniqueness for a fixed schedule, observation
  adequacy, and exact conditions for descent from instruction traces to
  reachable-state simulations. A finite family of unit event floors can share
  one charge. Retaining history makes a deterministic step injective without
  changing its observed transition. Those are mathematical answers with
  explicit premises. The repository contains the Coq kernel, an extracted
  OCaml runner, a Kami/Bluespec/Verilog hardware development, the monograph,
  and the mathematical specification. The assumption receipt covers 4,028
  statements: 2,979 are closed under the global context, 1,049 depend on
  standard-library axioms, and none depend on project-local or third-party
  axioms. Generic paid certification establishes accounting; semantic truth
  requires a sound checker. Hardware bridges retain their stated
  representation and compiler boundaries. The physical and foundational
  interpretations remain research questions. The software is licensed
  Apache-2.0. The monograph and distillation are licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0.
